DAY_DATA = {'day': 157, 'part1_intro': "Day 157 — Cymbal Retail — architecture defense — uses the roadmap's case or exam scope as the input. Each topic names the decision boundary, evidence owner and business effect; no product choice or score is treated as a fact without supporting evidence.", 'part2_intro': 'Trace the full reasoning path: supplied fact or requirement, candidate design or study rule, acceptance evidence, failure signal, owner and condition that would change the choice. The topology is a teaching model, not evidence of a live system or exam result.', 'part3_intro': 'These deep-dive cases preserve the original case or exam reasoning while separating supplied facts from architectural inference. The incident diagrams are synthetic exercises, not observed production events.', 'part4_intro': "The labs retain each day's original decision work, expanded into the eight execution stages used on Day 142. Complete them with prior artifacts and synthetic inputs unless an earlier roadmap page explicitly authorizes a cloud test.", 'exit_summary': 'A one-page design with quality/approval acceptance criteria, integration alternatives and cost assumptions.', 'arch_table_html': '\n<table><caption>Day-specific architecture and evidence path</caption><thead><tr><th>Topic boundary</th><th>Decision rule</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Compare secure modernization of legacy files, ETL, databases and containers</td><td>Separate supplied fact, requirement and proposed control.</td><td>One artifact, one rejected alternative and one changed-constraint result.</td><td>Local/tabletop evidence cannot prove production behavior or exam performance.</td></tr><tr><td>Justify each database, approval, observability and cost choice</td><td>Separate supplied fact, requirement and proposed control.</td><td>One artifact, one rejected alternative and one changed-constraint result.</td><td>Local/tabletop evidence cannot prove production behavior or exam performance.</td></tr></tbody></table>', 'arch_diagram': {'type': 'topology', 'title': 'Day 157: Cymbal Retail — architecture defense — evidence and decision path', 'desc': 'Three-tier architecture path for Cymbal Retail — architecture defense, showing case or exam inputs, requirements and trade-off evidence, then acceptance and ownership controls.', 'caption': 'Scope: a synthetic evidence path for Day 157 covering Compare secure modernization of legacy fil / Justify each database, approval, observabi. It shows reasoning boundaries and verification ownership; it does not prove a production deployment, exam result, price, quota, eligibility or customer outcome.', 'width': 1120, 'height': 690, 'layers': [{'name': 'TIER 1 · INPUTS / DEMAND', 'desc': 'case facts, prompts and constraints', 'x': 10, 'y': 55, 'w': 1100, 'h': 92, 'fill': '#0c2033', 'title_color': '#38bdf8', 'opacity': 0.72}, {'name': 'TIER 2 · EVIDENCE / REASONING', 'desc': 'requirements, alternatives and failure checks', 'x': 10, 'y': 185, 'w': 1100, 'h': 210, 'fill': '#241808', 'title_color': '#f59e0b', 'opacity': 0.64}, {'name': 'TIER 3 · GOVERNANCE / ACCEPTANCE', 'desc': 'owners, acceptance and revisit triggers', 'x': 10, 'y': 435, 'w': 1100, 'h': 115, 'fill': '#062316', 'title_color': '#22c55e', 'opacity': 0.68}], 'components': [{'name': 'Case or prompt', 'detail': 'Day 157 supplied scope', 'x': 60, 'y': 78, 'w': 250, 'h': 56, 'stroke': '#38bdf8', 'fill': '#102a42'}, {'name': 'Prior artifacts', 'detail': 'facts + decisions + errors', 'x': 435, 'y': 78, 'w': 250, 'h': 56, 'stroke': '#38bdf8', 'fill': '#102a42'}, {'name': 'Changed constraint', 'detail': 'cost / risk / scope', 'x': 810, 'y': 78, 'w': 250, 'h': 56, 'stroke': '#38bdf8', 'fill': '#102a42'}, {'name': 'Requirements map', 'detail': 'fact to acceptance', 'x': 60, 'y': 220, 'w': 250, 'h': 105, 'stroke': '#f59e0b', 'fill': '#3b2508'}, {'name': 'Option matrix', 'detail': 'choice + alternative', 'x': 435, 'y': 220, 'w': 250, 'h': 105, 'stroke': '#f59e0b', 'fill': '#3b2508'}, {'name': 'Failure rehearsal', 'detail': 'signal + safe response', 'x': 810, 'y': 220, 'w': 250, 'h': 105, 'stroke': '#f59e0b', 'fill': '#3b2508'}, {'name': 'Evidence owner', 'detail': 'source + artifact', 'x': 245, 'y': 455, 'w': 250, 'h': 70, 'stroke': '#22c55e', 'fill': '#064e3b'}, {'name': 'Acceptance gate', 'detail': 'observed / predicted', 'x': 625, 'y': 455, 'w': 250, 'h': 70, 'stroke': '#22c55e', 'fill': '#064e3b'}], 'flows': [{'x1': 185, 'y1': 134, 'x2': 185, 'y2': 220, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'extract'}, {'x1': 560, 'y1': 134, 'x2': 560, 'y2': 220, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'trace'}, {'x1': 935, 'y1': 134, 'x2': 935, 'y2': 220, 'type': 'warn', 'label': 'challenge'}, {'x1': 310, 'y1': 272, 'x2': 435, 'y2': 272, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'compare'}, {'x1': 685, 'y1': 272, 'x2': 810, 'y2': 272, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'rehearse'}, {'x1': 935, 'y1': 325, 'x2': 750, 'y2': 455, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'accept'}, {'x1': 560, 'y1': 325, 'x2': 370, 'y2': 455, 'type': 'warn', 'label': 'evidence'}, {'x1': 495, 'y1': 490, 'x2': 625, 'y2': 490, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'review'}], 'boundaries': [{'x': 42, 'y': 205, 'w': 1036, 'h': 140, 'color': '#f59e0b', 'label': 'EVIDENCE CHECK'}, {'x': 220, 'y': 442, 'w': 680, 'h': 94, 'color': '#22c55e', 'label': 'DECISION OWNER'}], 'probes': [{'cx': 185, 'cy': 106, 'label': 'PROBE 1 · input classified', 'badge': 'P1', 'color': '#38bdf8'}, {'cx': 560, 'cy': 272, 'label': 'PROBE 2 · trade-off recorded', 'badge': 'P2', 'color': '#f59e0b'}, {'cx': 750, 'cy': 490, 'label': 'PROBE 3 · acceptance result', 'badge': 'P3', 'color': '#22c55e'}]}, 'topics': [{'key': 'topic-01', 'title': 'Compare secure modernization of legacy files, ETL, databases and containers', 'overview': 'Defend a modernization path for Cymbal’s mixed environment. Compare staged coexistence, selective replacement and a broader rewrite using dependency, data freshness, rollback, security, team skills and cost—not a presumed seasonal-traffic or database requirement.', 'preview': 'Symptom: A team replaces an ETL path with a new service and disables the old parser. Effect: The compare secure modernization of legacy files, etl, databases and containers decision remains unverified.', 'technical': '<p>Map the migration by capability: supplier file intake, normalization/ETL, catalog storage, search/discovery, customer interaction, order entry and operations. A file transfer succeeding is not the same as a catalog update being accepted. Keep a reconciliation ledger with source file/version, row counts, checksums, rejected records and downstream publication state.</p><table><caption>Modernization choice points</caption><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Lower-risk use</th><th>Hidden cost</th><th>Acceptance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Strangler path</td><td>Preserve legacy contracts while replacing one capability.</td><td>Dual writes, routing and reconciliation.</td><td>Old/new results agree for a declared cohort.</td></tr><tr><td>Batch bridge</td><td>Stable supplier files with tolerable freshness.</td><td>Late files, duplicates and delayed correction.</td><td>Completeness, freshness and replay test.</td></tr><tr><td>Streaming/event path</td><td>Frequent changes with clear event ownership.</td><td>Ordering, duplicates, schema evolution and observability.</td><td>Idempotent replay and contract fixtures.</td></tr><tr><td>Database change</td><td>Only when access pattern, consistency or ownership requires it.</td><td>Migration, compatibility and operational learning.</td><td>Measured workload and rollback evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><a href="../sources.html#topic-048">Topic 048 source section</a>; current publisher material was checked 2026-09-30. Separate supplied facts, local observations and proposed controls.</p>', 'questions': ['Which requirement bounds the selected option?', 'What observable evidence supports the decision?', 'Which changed constraint would reverse the choice?', 'Who owns the failure response and review?'], 'reference': 'https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/v6.1_pca_cymbal_retail_case_study_english.pdf', 'reference_label': 'Official Topic 48 source (checked 2026-09-30)', 'scenario': {'scenario': 'A team replaces an ETL path with a new service and disables the old parser. Supplier files contain a field variation not represented in the new schema; products are silently skipped and search coverage falls.', 'impact': '', 'constraints': 'Use synthetic data, preserve the prior artifact and keep unknown behavior explicitly labeled.', 'facts': 'The case statement and artifact are teaching inputs; no production incident or learner result is claimed.', 'inference': 'The migration treated the legacy format as implementation detail rather than an external contract. Keep a compatibility boundary, quarantine invalid rows, measure completeness, run old/new in parallel for a cohort and retain rollback until reconciliation passes.', 'root': 'The migration treated the legacy format as implementation detail rather than an external contract. Keep a compatibility boundary, quarantine invalid rows, measure completeness, run old/new in parallel for a cohort and retain rollback until reconciliation passes.', 'fix': 'The migration treated the legacy format as implementation detail rather than an external contract. Keep a compatibility boundary, quarantine invalid rows, measure completeness, run old/new in parallel for a cohort and retain rollback until reconciliation passes.', 'verify': 'Re-run the bounded case, compare it with the declared acceptance signal and save the resulting decision trace.', 'residual': 'Parallel paths increase operational work. Set an explicit exit condition and owner for retiring the old flow.', 'diagram': ('supplied trigger', 'missing or untested control', 'business and operating impact', 'bounded defensive control', 'verified evidence artifact')}, 'lab': {'name': 'Compare a coexistence and replacement migration', 'goal': 'Create a Cymbal modernization ADR with a reversible wave and data-reconciliation acceptance.', 'expected': 'A migration comparison that names the first failure boundary, reconciliation evidence, rollback point and retirement condition.', 'mode': 'local/tabletop with synthetic data; no cloud access or spend', 'prereq': 'Bring the prior case or exam artifact and record it unavailable if missing.', 'preflight': 'Use `day-157-01-compare-a-coexistence-and-replacemen.md`; record the source, access date, prior artifact, owner and synthetic-data boundary.', 'steps': ['**Stage 1 — Preflight and validate assumptions/environment.** Create `day-157-01-compare-a-coexistence-and-replacemen.md`. Record Day 157, the prior artifact, the source `https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/v6.1_pca_cymbal_retail_case_study_english.pdf`, the access date, a named owner and the statement that the exercise uses synthetic or tabletop evidence only.', '**Stage 2 — Prepare the target, inputs, or backing resources.** Inventory. List file, ETL, database, container, search and order dependencies from Day 156.', '**Stage 3 — Author the plan, configuration, or analysis.** Draft paths. Describe a staged coexistence option and a replacement option with ownership and migration sequence.', '**Stage 4 — Execute or simulate the planned change.** Define reconciliation. Specify counts, checksums, rejected records, freshness, duplicates and customer-facing acceptance.', '**Stage 5 — Inspect expected state and verify outcomes.** Inject a fault. Send a late, duplicate or malformed supplier file; record quarantine, replay and rollback behavior.', '**Stage 6 — Rehearse a bounded failure, edge case, or decision challenge.** Choose. Select using evidence, skills, risk, cost and required freshness; name the condition that reverses the choice.', '**Stage 7 — Diagnose evidence and record remediation/decision.** Save. Record the ADR and wave acceptance in the daily artifact.', '**Stage 8 — Clean up or close out the exercise.** Save `day-157-01-compare-a-coexistence-and-replacemen.md`, link it from the daily artifact, keep the evidence, remove only disposable copies and state what needs an authorized or production verification.'], 'verification': 'A migration comparison that names the first failure boundary, reconciliation evidence, rollback point and retirement condition.', 'accept': 'The saved artifact identifies the requirement, selected and rejected options, acceptance evidence, failure response, owner and residual risk.', 'trouble': 'If the design says “migrate the database” without naming application dependencies and rollback, return to the capability map.', 'cleanup': 'Keep the evidence artifact and remove only disposable copies. No cloud resource, credential, payment detail or protected exam content belongs in this exercise.', 'file': 'day-157-01-compare-a-coexistence-and-replacemen.md'}}, {'key': 'topic-02', 'title': 'Justify each database, approval, observability and cost choice', 'overview': 'Database selection is downstream of access patterns, consistency, transaction boundaries, latency, recovery and team ownership. Likewise, observability should connect the catalog journey—not merely add a new dashboard beside Grafana, Nagios and Elastic.', 'preview': 'Symptom: The modernization adds a new catalog database, cache and observability stack, but teams cannot agree which record is authoritative. Effect: The justify each database, approval, observability and cost choice decision remains unverified.', 'technical': '<p>For every existing MySQL, SQL Server, Redis or MongoDB use, write the data model, read/write pattern, transaction need, TTL, scale shape and migration constraint. Do not infer that a distributed database is better without a requirement. For approval and discovery, trace user, associate, service account and supplier identities separately. Centralize useful events while preserving the owner’s ability to diagnose source, derived, review and publication states.</p><p>Cost analysis should include database instances, storage, replication, data transfer, ETL or event processing, search/indexing, model calls, review labor and duplicated coexistence. Record which inputs are measured, supplied or estimated and date current pricing checks.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Review question</strong><p>Can the proposed architecture explain one incorrect catalog item from supplier input to customer response, including who changed it, which version was published and how it is corrected?</p></div><p><a href="../sources.html#topic-048">Topic 048 source section</a>; current publisher material was checked 2026-09-30. Separate supplied facts, local observations and proposed controls.</p>', 'questions': ['Which requirement bounds the selected option?', 'What observable evidence supports the decision?', 'Which changed constraint would reverse the choice?', 'Who owns the failure response and review?'], 'reference': 'https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/v6.1_pca_cymbal_retail_case_study_english.pdf', 'reference_label': 'Official Topic 48 source (checked 2026-09-30)', 'scenario': {'scenario': 'The modernization adds a new catalog database, cache and observability stack, but teams cannot agree which record is authoritative. A generated attribute is corrected in one store and remains stale in another.', 'impact': '', 'constraints': 'Use synthetic data, preserve the prior artifact and keep unknown behavior explicitly labeled.', 'facts': 'The case statement and artifact are teaching inputs; no production incident or learner result is claimed.', 'inference': 'The proposal selected components without defining ownership and data contracts. Choose the smallest set of stores that satisfies the measured access patterns, define cache invalidation and source authority, and emit correlation IDs across file, API, review and publication paths.', 'root': 'The proposal selected components without defining ownership and data contracts. Choose the smallest set of stores that satisfies the measured access patterns, define cache invalidation and source authority, and emit correlation IDs across file, API, review and publication paths.', 'fix': 'The proposal selected components without defining ownership and data contracts. Choose the smallest set of stores that satisfies the measured access patterns, define cache invalidation and source authority, and emit correlation IDs across file, API, review and publication paths.', 'verify': 'Re-run the bounded case, compare it with the declared acceptance signal and save the resulting decision trace.', 'residual': 'Central observability can increase retention and ingestion cost. Set sampling, retention and alert ownership intentionally.', 'diagram': ('supplied trigger', 'missing or untested control', 'business and operating impact', 'bounded defensive control', 'verified evidence artifact')}, 'lab': {'name': 'Build a data-owner and observability decision table', 'goal': 'Tie database and monitoring choices to concrete access patterns, owners and acceptance signals.', 'expected': 'A defensible database and observability matrix grounded in access patterns, ownership, failure behavior and cost.', 'mode': 'local/tabletop with synthetic data; no cloud access or spend', 'prereq': 'Bring the prior case or exam artifact and record it unavailable if missing.', 'preflight': 'Use `day-157-02-build-a-data-owner-and-observability.md`; record the source, access date, prior artifact, owner and synthetic-data boundary.', 'steps': ['**Stage 1 — Preflight and validate assumptions/environment.** Create `day-157-02-build-a-data-owner-and-observability.md`. Record Day 157, the prior artifact, the source `https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/v6.1_pca_cymbal_retail_case_study_english.pdf`, the access date, a named owner and the statement that the exercise uses synthetic or tabletop evidence only.', '**Stage 2 — Prepare the target, inputs, or backing resources.** Classify data. For each store identify source/derived/cache data, consistency, retention and owner.', '**Stage 3 — Author the plan, configuration, or analysis.** Compare. Write two viable database/runtime combinations and one rejected choice; include migration and cost variables.', '**Stage 4 — Execute or simulate the planned change.** Trace a request. Follow a supplier correction through API, database, cache, review and customer read with a correlation ID.', '**Stage 5 — Inspect expected state and verify outcomes.** Inject inconsistency. Make the cache or derived store stale. Record detection, invalidation and customer-safe behavior.', '**Stage 6 — Rehearse a bounded failure, edge case, or decision challenge.** Set observability. Choose signals for completeness, freshness, approval backlog, errors, latency, spend and security; assign owners.', '**Stage 7 — Diagnose evidence and record remediation/decision.** Save. Append the decision table and a dated pricing/limit question list.', '**Stage 8 — Clean up or close out the exercise.** Save `day-157-02-build-a-data-owner-and-observability.md`, link it from the daily artifact, keep the evidence, remove only disposable copies and state what needs an authorized or production verification.'], 'verification': 'A defensible database and observability matrix grounded in access patterns, ownership, failure behavior and cost.', 'accept': 'The saved artifact identifies the requirement, selected and rejected options, acceptance evidence, failure response, owner and residual risk.', 'trouble': 'If the only justification is “recommended service,” state the workload requirement that makes it fit or mark it unresolved.', 'cleanup': 'Keep the evidence artifact and remove only disposable copies. No cloud resource, credential, payment detail or protected exam content belongs in this exercise.', 'file': 'day-157-02-build-a-data-owner-and-observability.md'}}]}
